y", "Infectious Aetiology: high WBC with normal differential and elevated LAP suggests non-bacterial cause", "Metastatic Cancer: abdominal pain and hypercalcemia could indicate visceral mass, but lacks localized findings"], "alarm_features": ["Unintentional weight loss", "Leukocytosis with elevated LAP", "Hypercalcemia", "Elevated BUN (20 mg/dL) and borderline creatinine"]}, "branches": {"B1": {"id": "B1", "label": "Myeloid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "live", "prior": 0.3815415821501014, "posterior": 0.5006321112515802, "danger": 0.8, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": ["Elevated LAP is more characteristic of myeloid neoplasms (e.g., chronic myeloid leukemia in blast crisis) than leukemoid reactions, which typically show low LA…"], "evidence_against": ["Myeloid neoplasms with increased blasts typically exhibit abnormal peripheral blood differentials. The normal differential here weakens B1 as a primary diagnos…"], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.75, "interrupt_relevance": 0.7, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "mechanism", "representative_diseases": ["acute myeloid leukemia", "myelodysplastic syndrome with excess blasts", "chronic myeloid leukemia in blast crisis"]}, "B2": {"id": "B2", "label": "Lymphoid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "live", "prior": 0.13488843813387422, "posterior": 0.08849557522123892, "danger": 0.8, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": ["Severe lymphoproliferative disorders (B2/B3) usually show peripheral blasts or bone marrow abnormalities. The normal differential here argues against these."], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.7, "interrupt